      <section class="abstract">
        <h2>Abstract</h2>
        <p><b>Question.</b> Indonesia's rice harvested area is measured by a field survey with a
        two-month lag. Sentinel-1 sees through monsoon cloud and could in principle measure the same
        quantity as it happens. We ask what a published radar paddy detector actually recovers, and
        whether the reason it falls short is the one its own page gives.</p>
        <p><b>Method.</b> {num(A.cells)} cells of {A.cell_m} m over {A.kabupaten.length} kabupaten in
        {A.provinces.join(", ")}, {A.sar_window[0]}–{A.sar_window[1]}, benchmarked against BPS's
        area-frame survey (KSA) and, independently, against a published 10 m rice map whose own
        single/double/triple classes carry a cropping intensity. All statistics below are recomputed
        from the case's own stored output; one controlled experiment removes radar looks from a single
        regency and re-runs the unchanged detector.</p>
        <p><b>Findings.</b> The benchmark is not the moving target: the survey's 2018 method change
        ({A.benchmark.break_pct}%) is {2022 - A.benchmark.break_year} years before the record opens, the
        five-regency series varies by only {A.benchmark.five_unit_cv_pct}% over the whole survey era,
        and the independent satellite map lands within <b>{Math.abs(A.benchmark.map_vs_ksa_pct).toFixed(1)}%</b>
        of it. The shortfall is real and it factors: detected area is
        <b>{pct(H.ratio_last, 0)}</b> of the benchmark in {last.year}, the product of finding
        <b>{pct(H.recall_extent_last, 0)}</b> of the fields and
        <b>{pct(H.recall_ci_last, 0)}</b> of the crops on the fields it finds — so
        <b>{pct(H.share_extent, 0)}</b> of the deficit is missing fields, not missing crops. On cells
        the independent map calls double-cropping the detector returns <b>{cc["2"].cond.toFixed(2)}</b>
        crops, not one; it detects only <b>{pct(cc["1"].detect_rate, 1)}</b> of single-cropping cells and
        <b>{pct(cc["3"].detect_rate, 1)}</b> of triple-cropping ones. The deficit is not in the second
        season either: once a systematic {A.seasonal.lag_months_removed}-month late bias is removed, the
        two harvest lobes are recovered at {pct(A.seasonal.lobes_lag_corrected.rendeng.ratio, 0)} and
        {pct(A.seasonal.lobes_lag_corrected.gadu.ratio, 0)}. What does predict detection is revisit:
        thinning {A.thinning.kabupaten}'s own record from {lad0.median_gap_days} to
        {lad1.median_gap_days} days between looks, with the fields, the detector and the thresholds
        unchanged, costs <b>{pct(1 - lad1.cycles_vs_full, 0)}</b> of its crop cycles and
        <b>{pct(1 - lad1.extent_vs_full, 0)}</b> of its fields — and reproduces the East Java
        regencies' observed shortfall. As Sentinel-1C and 1D came online inside the sample,
        {esc(bestGain.kabupaten)}'s gap fell {bestGain.gap_first}→{bestGain.gap_last} days and its
        recall rose <b>×{bestGain.gain.toFixed(2)}</b>. Finally, the calibrated series the case
        reports at R² {A.calibration.r2_annual_calibrated.toFixed(2)} on the annual panel has a monthly
        R² of <b>{A.calibration.r2_monthly_calibrated.toFixed(2)}</b>, and the satellite contributes
        <b>{pct(A.calibration.mean_sat_share, 1)}</b> of its hectares — negatively in
        {A.calibration.negative_units.length} of {A.kabupaten.length} regencies.</p>
        <p><b>Conclusion.</b> This is a <i>sampling</i> instrument, not a <i>resolution</i> one. The
        case attributes every failure to cell size and proposes a smaller cell; the evidence says the
        binding constraint is how often the satellite looks. The correct product is not a hectare
        count at all — it is the harvest date, which the same detector already gets to within one
        systematic month with a shape correlation of {A.timing.median_xcorr_at_lag.toFixed(2)}.</p>
      </section>

