      <section id="s1">
        <h2><span class="num">1</span> The claim under test</h2>
        <p>Indonesia measures rice harvested area with the KSA area sampling frame: enumerators
        classify the crop stage at fixed sample points every month, and the classification is grossed
        up to a regency figure published about two months later. It is a good survey. It is also slow,
        it cannot be interrogated below its own sample density, and it is the number on which rice
        import decisions, procurement targets and the national self-sufficiency programme are argued.</p>
        <p>Radar is the obvious challenger. Java's rice grows through the monsoon under cloud that
        defeats optical sensors, and the crop calendar is written directly into the backscatter:
        a flooded field before transplanting is a mirror and collapses the signal, a tillering canopy
        is a volume of vertical scatterers and lifts it, and the crop is cut and it drops again. The
        case under review implements exactly that, over {num(A.cells)} cells of {A.cell_m} m and
        {A.seasons.length} seasons, and publishes {A.checks_passed} of {A.checks.length} checks
        passed.</p>
        <p>Publishing five failures is the right instinct, and it means the <i>diagnosis</i> is the
        product. The page's diagnosis is a single sentence: <i>"we find the rice; we find one crop
        where there are two."</i> That sentence makes an arithmetically testable claim, because
        harvested area is a flow and factors exactly:</p>
        <p class="pull">harvested area = the fields that grew rice × the crops each field grew</p>
        <p>If the diagnosis is right, the first factor is near complete and the second is near half.
        Sections 5 to 7 measure both.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s2">
        <h2><span class="num">2</span> Prior art, and what a working system looks like</h2>
        <p>Radar rice mapping is not new and it is not speculative. <a href="#ref-nelson">Nelson et al.
        (2014)</a>, reporting the RIICE programme, mapped {LIT.riice.mapped_mha} million hectares of rice
        across {LIT.riice.footprints} footprints in {LIT.riice.countries} Asian countries from
        {LIT.riice.images} X-band images, with classification accuracies of {LIT.riice.acc_lo}–{LIT.riice.acc_hi}%
        against more than 1,300 field observations. <a href="#ref-torbick">Torbick et al. (2017)</a> is
        the closer comparator, because it is C-band Sentinel-1 over a tropical monsoon country: from
        {LIT.torbick.images} {LIT.torbick.sensor} images they mapped {num(LIT.torbick.area_ha)} ha of
        harvested rice across {LIT.torbick.country} and reported <b>R² = {LIT.torbick.r2}</b> against the
        government census.</p>
        <p>The third benchmark is not a paper but a product we can test against pixel by pixel.
        <a href="#ref-opensea">Open-SEA-Rice-10</a> (Ginting et al., 2025) maps Southeast Asian rice at
        10 m for {A.mask.year} with an overall accuracy of {pct(LIT.opensea.oa, 1)} and an F1 of
        {LIT.opensea.f1}, and — decisively for this review — labels every rice pixel
        <i>single</i>, <i>double</i> or <i>triple</i> crop. Its harvested-area estimates agree with
        official statistics at R² {LIT.opensea.sea_r2} region-wide and R² {LIT.opensea.idn_r2} for
        Indonesia. It is therefore simultaneously an independent extent map <i>and</i> an independent
        statement about cropping intensity, made by other people, from a different method.</p>
        <p>Set against that literature, this case's uncalibrated result — R²
        {A.g1.uncalibrated.kabupaten_r2} against the official survey, and
        {A.g1.uncalibrated.aggregate_by_year[0].diff_pct}% on the {A.g1.uncalibrated.aggregate_by_year[0].year}
        aggregate — is not a marginal miss. It is a different order of outcome from published work on
        the same sensor, and that is what makes the diagnosis worth testing rather than accepting.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s3">
        <h2><span class="num">3</span> Data, method, and what the instrument published</h2>
        <p><b>Radar.</b> Sentinel-1 radiometrically terrain-corrected γ⁰ at 10 m, read at 80 m and
        aggregated to a {A.cell_m} m analysis cell, {num(A.acquisitions)} acquisitions across
        {A.kabupaten.length} regencies, orbits normalised separately and never mixed, averaged in
        linear power.</p>
        <p><b>Detector.</b> A local minimum of VV at least {A.thresholds.flood_drop_db} dB below the
        cell's own {A.thresholds.flood_baseline_pctl}th-percentile baseline marks transplanting; a VH
        rise of at least {A.thresholds.rise_db} dB within {A.thresholds.rise_window_days} days confirms
        it is a crop; the following VH maximum is heading; harvest follows. Events whose defining dates
        sit inside an observation gap longer than {A.thresholds.max_gap_days} days are refused.</p>
        <p><b>Benchmarks.</b> BPS monthly KSA harvested area per regency for West and East Java, annual
        for Central Java; and Open-SEA-Rice-10 as an independent extent and intensity map.</p>
        <div class="checks">
          {A.checks.map((c: any) => (
            <div class={`chk ${c.pass ? "ok" : "no"}`}>
              <span class="cn">Check {c.n}</span>
              <span class="cname">{c.name}</span>
              <span class="cdet">{c.detail}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="caveat"><b>Where this review agrees with the case.</b> The thresholds were fixed
        before results and are not tuned here either. The scope is honestly stated. The flow/stock
        distinction — that a field yielding two crops contributes its area twice — is correctly
        implemented, and it is the reason the shortfall can be factored at all. Nothing below is a
        complaint about rigour; it is a disagreement about a diagnosis.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s4">
        <h2><span class="num">4</span> Finding one — the benchmark is not the moving target</h2>
        <p>The first hypothesis any reviewer should test is that the official series moved rather than
        the satellite failing. BPS replaced a generation of eye-estimates with the KSA area frame from
        reference year {A.benchmark.break_year}, and that break is real and large: national harvested
        area steps from {(A.benchmark.pre_ksa_ha / 1e6).toFixed(2)} Mha under the old method to
        {(A.benchmark.first_ksa_ha / 1e6).toFixed(2)} Mha under the new one, a change of
        <b>{A.benchmark.break_pct}%</b>, with {2016}–{2017} a moratorium carrying no data at all.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F2} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Top: three measurements of harvested area over the same six
          regencies in {A.benchmark.map_vs_ksa_year}. Bottom: the national official series, with the
          {A.benchmark.break_year} method change drawn as the break it is, and the point at which this
          case's radar record opens.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>But the break cannot explain this shortfall, because it is {2022 - A.benchmark.break_year}
        years before the radar record opens. Inside the window the benchmark is quiet: over the whole
        survey era the five regencies with a continuous series move within
        <b>{A.benchmark.five_unit_range_pct}%</b> peak to trough, a coefficient of variation of
        {A.benchmark.five_unit_cv_pct}%.</p>
        <p>The stronger test is corroboration by something that is not a survey at all. Summing
        Open-SEA-Rice-10's own crop classes over exactly these six regencies gives
        <b>{num(A.benchmark.map_implied_ha)} ha</b> of harvested area against the survey's
        {num(dec.find((d: any) => d.year === A.benchmark.map_vs_ksa_year)?.ksa_ha ?? 0)} ha — a gap of
        <b>{A.benchmark.map_vs_ksa_pct > 0 ? "+" : ""}{A.benchmark.map_vs_ksa_pct}%</b>. An area-frame
        field survey and a 10 m satellite product built by unrelated authors agree to within a point
        and a half. The benchmark is sound, and the {pct(1 - H.ratio_last, 0)} that is missing is ours.</p>
        <p class="caveat"><b>One honest caution on the corroboration.</b> Open-SEA-Rice-10 derives its
        crop count from NDVI peaks in optical imagery, which is the method this case exists to avoid in
        a cloudy country. It is therefore an independent benchmark for <i>extent</i> without
        qualification, and an independent benchmark for <i>intensity</i> that shares no method with the
        survey but does share a known weakness. That it lands within
        {Math.abs(A.benchmark.map_vs_ksa_pct).toFixed(1)}% of KSA anyway is what makes it usable here.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s5">
        <h2><span class="num">5</span> Finding two — the shortfall factors, and it factors the other way</h2>
        <p>Because harvested area is a flow, the ratio of our figure to the benchmark is exactly the
        product of two recalls: the share of the rice fields we find at all, and the share of each
        field's crops we count where we do find it. Both are measurable against the independent map,
        and neither has been reported.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F1} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Every regency-year plotted by its two recalls. Dashed curves are
          contours of constant agreement with the benchmark. The stated diagnosis — all the fields, half
          the crops — predicts the marked point on the right-hand edge. Not one observation is near it.
          Hover any dot.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>In {last.year} the instrument reports <b>{pct(last.ratio_ksa, 0)}</b> of the benchmark. That
        is {pct(last.recall_extent, 0)} of the fields multiplied by {pct(last.recall_ci, 0)} of the
        crops. Averaged over the three complete years, <b>{pct(H.share_extent, 0)} of the deficit is the
        extent term</b> and only {pct(1 - H.share_extent, 0)} is the intensity term. The detector is not
        finding the rice and missing a season. It is missing most of the rice.</p>
        <p>The per-regency picture is starker than the aggregate. {esc(worstKab.kabupaten)} in
        {worstKab.year} recovers <b>{pct(worstKab.recall_extent, 1)}</b> of its rice fields —
        {pct(worstKab.ratio_ksa, 1)} of the benchmark — while still counting
        {worstKab.det_ci.toFixed(2)} crops on the few fields it does find.</p>
        <p>The case's own page already carries the number that says this, and reads it the other way
        round. It quotes <b>{pct(A.g4.agreement_on_ours, 0)}</b> agreement "on our own area" as evidence
        that the rice is found. That figure is a <i>precision</i>, and precision is the one statistic a
        detector can always buy by detecting less. Recomputed per year rather than pooled across four
        seasons against a one-year map, precision is
        {pct(prfP[0], 0)}–{pct(prfP[1], 0)} and recall is
        <b>{pct(prfR[0], 0)}–{pct(prfR[1], 0)}</b>. Both numbers are true;
        only one of them is the answer to "do we find the rice".</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s6">
        <h2><span class="num">6</span> Finding three — asked in the map's own units, the diagnosis fails again</h2>
        <p>The independent map labels each rice cell with the number of crops it grows. That converts
        the diagnosis into a prediction with no wriggle room: on cells the map calls double-cropping,
        a detector that "finds one crop where there are two" should return about one.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F3} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Left: the share of each class's cells in which we detect any crop
          cycle at all. Right: the number of cycles we count in the cells where we detect one, against
          the map's own crop count for that class (the pale bar).</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>On double-cropping cells the detector returns <b>{cc["2"].cond.toFixed(2)}</b> cycles, not
        one — {pct(cc["2"].cond / 2, 0)} of the truth, which is a real shortfall but not the stated one.
        It also separates the classes far less than it should: the ratio of what it counts on
        double-cropping cells to what it counts on single-cropping cells is
        <b>{cc.ratio_cond_2_over_1.toFixed(2)}</b> where the map says {cc.ratio_map_2_over_1}, and on
        triple-cropping cells it counts {cc["3"].cond.toFixed(2)} against three.</p>
        <p>The left panel is the finding that matters. The detector sees
        <b>{pct(cc["2"].detect_rate, 1)}</b> of double-cropping cells,
        <b>{pct(cc["1"].detect_rate, 1)}</b> of single-cropping cells and
        <b>{pct(cc["3"].detect_rate, 1)}</b> of triple-cropping cells. It is not a rice detector with a
        counting problem; it is a detector of one particular signature — deeply flooded, synchronously
        transplanted, irrigated double-crop paddy — that is nearly blind to everything else rice does in
        Java. Single-cropped rice is largely rainfed and often direct-seeded, so it never presents the
        standing-water minimum the first rule requires; triple-cropped fields turn over faster than the
        {A.thresholds.min_cycle_days}-day minimum cycle allows.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s7">
        <h2><span class="num">7</span> Finding four — nor is the deficit in the second season</h2>
        <p>"One crop where there are two" has a seasonal signature. Java's calendar is bimodal: the
        wet-season <i>rendeng</i> harvest peaks around March, the dry-season <i>gadu</i> harvest around
        August. A detector that finds the first crop and misses the second should recover the rendeng
        lobe well and the gadu lobe badly.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F4} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Monthly harvest as a share of the year, ours against the official
          survey, with our curve also shown corrected for the systematic
          {A.seasonal.lag_months_removed}-month late bias measured in §12. The bars below are the share
          of each month's official harvest we recover.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>As published, the pattern is the opposite of the prediction: we recover
        {pct(A.seasonal.lobes.rendeng.ratio, 0)} of the rendeng harvest and
        <b>{pct(A.seasonal.lobes.gadu.ratio, 0)}</b> of the gadu harvest — the second season is
        recovered {A.seasonal.lobes.gadu_over_rendeng_ratio.toFixed(2)} times <i>better</i> than the
        first. That comparison is contaminated, because the detector's harvest dates run late and a late
        bias moves mass out of the first lobe into the second. Removing the measured
        {A.seasonal.lag_months_removed}-month lag flattens it: rendeng
        {pct(A.seasonal.lobes_lag_corrected.rendeng.ratio, 0)}, gadu
        {pct(A.seasonal.lobes_lag_corrected.gadu.ratio, 0)}, a ratio of
        <b>{A.seasonal.lobes_lag_corrected.gadu_over_rendeng_ratio.toFixed(2)}</b>.</p>
        <p>Either way the conclusion is the same and it is the one the diagnosis forbids: the deficit is
        <b>flat across the calendar</b>. Whatever the detector is missing, it is not a season.</p>
        <p>The shoulder months are the exception and they are diagnostic rather than encouraging. January,
        June, November and December carry {pct(A.seasonal.lobes.shoulder.share_ksa, 0)} of the official harvest but
        {pct(A.seasonal.lobes.shoulder.share_ours, 0)} of ours, and we "recover"
        {pct(A.seasonal.lobes.shoulder.ratio, 0)} of them — more than either real lobe. That is not
        detection; it is the late bias of §12 smearing genuine February–May harvests into the months
        after them, and it is why a monthly comparison flatters this instrument in exactly the months
        nobody is procuring against.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s8">
        <h2><span class="num">8</span> Finding five — the decisive test: same fields, fewer looks</h2>
        <p>If the deficit is neither a season nor a crop count, what sets it? The observable that moves
        with it across regencies is how often the satellite looked. The problem with a cross-regency
        comparison is that everything else moves too — soil, variety, plot size, irrigation command. So
        we ran the experiment that holds all of it fixed.</p>
        <p>We took {esc(A.thinning.kabupaten)}, the densest record in the sample
        ({lad0.n_acquisitions} looks, {lad0.median_gap_days} days apart), removed looks from its own
        time series, rebuilt the series through what survived, and re-ran the identical detector with
        identical thresholds. Nothing about the fields changed. Only the number of looks did.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F5} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> The chartreuse line is {esc(A.thinning.kabupaten)} with looks
          progressively removed, pooled over all {A.seasons.length} seasons. The small dots are the six
          regencies as they actually are, one per calendar year, so they sit lower than the curve for
          the same gap; the shape, not the level, is the comparison. Hover any point.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Halving the looks — {lad0.median_gap_days} days between them to {lad1.median_gap_days} —
        costs <b>{pct(1 - lad1.cycles_vs_full, 0)} of the crop cycles</b> and
        <b>{pct(1 - lad1.extent_vs_full, 0)} of the fields</b>. At {lad[2].median_gap_days} days
        {pct(lad[2].extent_recall_vs_prior, 1)} of the fields survive; at {lad[3].median_gap_days} days,
        {pct(lad[3].extent_recall_vs_prior, 2)}. Detection is not a slope in revisit. It is a cliff, and
        the edge sits between eight and twelve days — exactly where the East Java regencies sit.</p>
        <p class="pull">Thinned to a {lad1.median_gap_days}-day gap, {esc(A.thinning.kabupaten)}'s own
        fields yield {pct(lad1.extent_recall_vs_prior, 1)} recall. Bojonegoro, at a
        {A.revisit.rows.find((r: any) => r.kabupaten === "Bojonegoro" && r.year === 2023)?.median_gap_days}-day
        gap, yields {pct(A.revisit.rows.find((r: any) => r.kabupaten === "Bojonegoro" && r.year === 2023)?.recall_extent ?? 0, 1)}.
        The experiment reproduces the case's own cross-section from revisit alone.</p>
        <p>There is a second, natural experiment in the same data, and it runs the other way. Sentinel-1B
        failed on {A.revisit.s1b_failure}, six months <i>before</i> this record opens, so the whole series
        sits on the degraded side of that event and the obvious before/after test is unavailable. But
        Sentinel-1C and 1D came into service <i>during</i> it, and revisit rose.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F6} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Share of rice fields found in {A.full_years[0]} (grey) against
          {A.full_years[A.full_years.length - 1]} (chartreuse), with each regency's change in revisit gap.
          Regencies whose gap did not change gained little; the one whose gap fell furthest gained most.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>{esc(bestGain.kabupaten)}'s median gap fell from {bestGain.gap_first} to
        {bestGain.gap_last} days and its recall rose <b>×{bestGain.gain.toFixed(2)}</b>, from
        {pct(bestGain.recall_first, 1)} to {pct(bestGain.recall_last, 1)} — with no change to the
        detector, the thresholds, the crop or the fields. Across all
        {A.revisit.rows.length} regency-years the correlation between revisit gap and recall is
        <b>{A.revisit.corr_gap_vs_recall}</b>.</p>
        <p class="caveat"><b>What the thinning test does not do.</b> It rebuilds the series by
        interpolating through the surviving looks rather than re-deriving it from the raw scenes, so it
        reproduces {pct(A.thinning.baseline_vs_published, 0)} of the published cycle count at full
        density. Every rung is built the same way, so the ladder is internally consistent and the
        comparison against the observed regencies is like for like — but a full re-ingest at reduced
        density would be the cleaner version, and the absolute level of each rung should be read as
        indicative rather than exact.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s9">
        <h2><span class="num">9</span> Finding six — the "damping" the case measures is largely revisit</h2>
        <p>The case has a mechanism for the shortfall and it is a good one: a {A.cell_m} m cell holds
        several 0.3–0.5 ha paddy plots that are not transplanted on the same day, so the cell mean never
        swings as far as a single plot does, and the second cycle is averaged away. It measures that
        damping as the cell's seasonal VH range, builds it into the calibration as an interaction term,
        and reads the cross-section off it.</p>
        <p>That mechanism makes a prediction the thinning test can check. If the canopy swing measures
        plot heterogeneity, removing looks should barely move it.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F7} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> The seasonal canopy swing against revisit. The chartreuse line
          is {esc(A.thinning.kabupaten)} with looks removed; the small dots are each regency as observed,
          plotted at its own mean gap.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Thinning {esc(A.thinning.kabupaten)} from {lad0.median_gap_days} to {lad[3].median_gap_days}
        days moves its canopy swing from {lad0.vh_range_db.toFixed(2)} to {lad[3].vh_range_db.toFixed(2)}
        dB — {(lad0.vh_range_db - lad[3].vh_range_db).toFixed(2)} dB — while costing
        {pct(1 - lad[3].extent_vs_full, 1)} of its fields. So the swing does respond to sampling, but
        weakly; it cannot be the channel through which revisit destroys detection.</p>
        <p>Two conclusions follow, and they point in different directions. First, the case's damping
        term is not the mechanism: what fewer looks destroy is the <i>shape</i> the detector tests for —
        a local minimum, then a {A.thresholds.rise_db} dB rise inside
        {A.thresholds.rise_window_days} days — not the amplitude it never tests directly. Second, the
        East Java regencies do sit genuinely below the thinning curve on swing
        ({Math.min(...Object.values(A.revisit.vh_range_db).map(Number)).toFixed(2)} dB against
        {lad1.vh_range_db.toFixed(2)} dB for {esc(A.thinning.kabupaten)} thinned to the same gap), so
        there <i>is</i> a real amplitude difference on top of the sampling one. The case's error is not
        that the damping is imaginary. It is that damping was made to carry a cross-section that revisit
        explains.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s10">
        <h2><span class="num">10</span> Finding seven — the calibrated series is almost entirely not the satellite</h2>
        <p>The case reports a calibrated R² of
        {A.calibration.r2_annual_calibrated.toFixed(2)} against the official survey. The case is careful to publish
        the uncalibrated result beside it and to fail the check on that basis, which is exactly right.
        It is still worth saying what the calibrated number is.</p>
        <p>The calibration is fitted on {A.calibration.n_fit_rows} regency-<i>month</i> rows. At that
        resolution — the resolution it was fitted at — its R² against the survey is
        <b>{A.calibration.r2_monthly_calibrated.toFixed(2)}</b>. The {A.calibration.r2_annual_calibrated.toFixed(2)} is what
        appears after twelve monthly predictions are summed into a year, which is mostly a statement
        that regencies differ in size.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F8} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 8.</b> How much of each regency's calibrated hectares the satellite
          measurement contributes. The rest is the fitted constant and covariates that do not vary within
          a regency.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Across the panel the detected area supplies <b>{pct(A.calibration.mean_sat_share, 1)}</b> of
        the calibrated hectares. Worse, the interaction that carries the damping story gives the detected
        area an effective slope of {A.calibration.coefficients.detected_kha} −
        {Math.abs(A.calibration.coefficients.detected_kha_x_inv_vh_range * 10).toFixed(2)} ÷ swing, which
        <b>changes sign below {A.calibration.sign_flip_vh_range_db} dB</b>. Three of the six regencies —
        {A.calibration.negative_units.join(", ")} — sit below that line, so the fitted model says that in
        those regencies the more rice the satellite finds, the less rice the survey will report. That is
        not a calibration; it is a set of regency constants with a sign error attached.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s11">
        <h2><span class="num">11</span> Finding eight — the detector sits on a knife-edge it does not declare</h2>
        <p>The case publishes a threshold-sensitivity table, which is more than most do. Read as a
        stability statement rather than a disclosure, it is alarming.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F9} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 9.</b> Detected hectares under each threshold variant, logarithmic scale.
          The published setting is marked.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Moving the canopy-rise threshold by one decibel in either direction changes the answer by
        <b>{riseUp > 0 ? "+" : ""}{pct(riseUp, 0)}</b> and <b>{pct(riseDn, 0)}</b>. That is not a
        robustness range; it is the whole result. The reason is visible in the case's own diagnostics:
        the median detected rise is {vhRiseLo.toFixed(2)}–{vhRiseHi.toFixed(2)} dB against a threshold of
        {A.thresholds.rise_db.toFixed(1)} dB, so half of all detections sit within a decibel of
        rejection. A speckle realisation, an incidence-angle residual or a missing look is enough to move
        an event across the line — which is precisely why removing looks in §8 is so destructive.</p>
        <p class="caveat">The literature's unchanged single-plot rule (VV below
        {A.thresholds.flood_db} dB) yields {num(sens["literature -17 dB absolute, unchanged"])} ha where
        the scale-adapted criterion yields {num(sensBase)} ha. The case is right that this is a fact
        about the analysis cell rather than about Indonesian rice, and right to publish it. But the
        restated criterion inherits the fragility rather than curing it.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s12">
        <h2><span class="num">12</span> Finding nine — the one output that works is the one presented as a failure</h2>
        <p>The timing check fails at a median error of {A.g2.median_abs_error_weeks} weeks against a
        {A.g2.threshold_weeks}-week threshold, and the case attributes part of that to the detector
        locking onto the wrong lobe of a bimodal harvest. That attribution is testable: an argmax of a
        two-peaked curve is brittle, but the whole curve is not. We re-scored the same regency-years by
        sliding our monthly harvest curve around the calendar and taking the shift that best matches the
        survey's.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F10} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 10.</b> Left: the whole-curve lag, in months, across the
          {A.timing.n} regency-years with a monthly benchmark. Right: median correlation with the
          official curve as published, and after removing that lag.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>The shape-based estimator gives the same median as the argmax —
        {A.timing.median_abs_xcorr_lag_months} month against
        {A.timing.median_abs_argmax_error_months} — so the median failure is not an artefact of the
        estimator. What the distribution adds is that the failure has two modes, not one.
        <b>{modal.n} of {A.timing.n}</b> regency-years sit at a clean
        {modal.lag > 0 ? "+" : ""}{modal.lag}-month lag; {aliased} sit four or more months away, which
        on a bimodal calendar is the lobe alias the case describes. So both explanations are partly
        right — and the dominant one is a <b>systematic, one-signed, one-month late bias</b>. Once that
        single constant is removed, the correlation between our monthly harvest curve and the official
        one rises from <b>{A.timing.median_xcorr_at_zero.toFixed(2)}</b> to
        <b>{A.timing.median_xcorr_at_lag.toFixed(2)}</b>.</p>
        <p class="pull">The instrument that cannot count hectares can tell you, three weeks before the
        survey can, that the harvest is arriving early or late. That is a different product, and it is
        the one worth selling.</p>
        <p>A systematic bias is a calibration constant, not an error; the estimated harvest date is
        heading plus {A.thresholds.head_to_harvest_days} days, and heading is dated from a maximum that
        a sparse series finds late. The correction is one number and it is estimable from the same
        panel. The hectare count, by contrast, needs a different satellite tasking regime.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s13">
        <h2><span class="num">13</span> What follows for decisions</h2>
        <p>Indonesian rice numbers are not academic. BPS's KSA figure feeds the Ministry of
        Agriculture's production accounting, Bulog's procurement and stock targets, and Badan Pangan
        Nasional's advice on whether to import — a decision taken on a scale of hundreds of thousands to
        millions of tonnes, months ahead of the harvest it is meant to cover. An instrument that claims
        to see the harvest before the survey does is claiming to move that decision.</p>
        <ol class="uses">
          <li><b>Not: an independent hectare count.</b> At {pct(H.ratio_last, 0)} of the benchmark
          uncalibrated, and {A.calibration.mean_sat_share > 0 ? pct(A.calibration.mean_sat_share, 0) : ""}
          satellite content once calibrated, this cannot referee a national area figure and should not be
          offered as a second opinion on one. The independent 10 m map already does that job better and
          agrees with the survey to {Math.abs(A.benchmark.map_vs_ksa_pct).toFixed(1)}%.</li>
          <li><b>Yes: harvest timing, with the bias removed.</b> A shape correlation of
          {A.timing.median_xcorr_at_lag.toFixed(2)} at a fixed one-month offset is a usable early-warning signal for
          when the peak lands, which is the input procurement scheduling actually needs and the one the
          survey delivers late. This is the product.</li>
          <li><b>Yes: a revisit-adequacy map.</b> The single most decision-relevant output of this review
          is that a {lad1.median_gap_days}-day gap costs {pct(1 - lad1.extent_vs_full, 0)} of the fields.
          A map of where Java is and is not observed densely enough to monitor is worth having before
          anyone commissions monitoring, and this pipeline can produce it today.</li>
          <li><b>Yes: disagreement as a triage list.</b> Cells the independent map calls rice and the
          radar never sees are either a detector blind spot or a real change in what is grown there. That
          list is a survey design input, and it is currently discarded.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section id="s14">
        <h2><span class="num">14</span> What remains open</h2>
        <ol class="next">
          <li><b>Re-ingest at reduced density, properly.</b> §8's ladder interpolates through surviving
          looks rather than re-deriving the series from scenes. Re-running the ingest with orbits
          withheld would give the same curve without the caveat, and would also separate "fewer dates"
          from "fewer orbits", which the current test conflates.</li>
          <li><b>A detector that does not need a hard threshold.</b> §11 shows half of all detections sit
          within a decibel of rejection. A likelihood or shape-matching formulation over the whole cycle,
          rather than three sequential thresholds, would degrade gracefully with sampling instead of
          falling off a cliff — and is the change most likely to make the East Java regencies work.</li>
          <li><b>Estimate and publish the timing bias.</b> One constant, estimated on the calibration
          years, scored on the hold-out. It converts §12 from a finding into a product.</li>
          <li><b>Ask BPS for the KSA phase labels.</b> The survey's own <i>vegetatif / generatif /
          persiapan lahan</i> classification is the exact label set this detector wants, and it is
          published through the API for one province with almost no rice. A data request costs a letter
          and would turn every one of these checks into a supervised problem.</li>
          <li><b>Test the smaller cell against the more frequent look.</b> The case proposes 20–30 m
          cells as the fix. This review's evidence says revisit binds first. Both are testable on the
          same six regencies, and the answer decides what a pilot should buy.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

